      overview: "A Takaful company acts as trustee/manager (Wakalah or Mudarabah basis) over a fund built from participant contributions, paying claims from the fund and distributing any underwriting surplus to participants.",
      simpleExplanation: "Participants pay into a shared fund; the Takaful company manages that fund either as an agent (Wakalah, earning a fee) or as a partner (Mudarabah, sharing profit); claims are paid out of the fund, and whatever is left over after claims - the underwriting surplus - belongs to the participants as a group, not the company, while any investment profit made on the fund is shared per the agreed basis.",
      academicExplanation: "A Takaful company serves as trustee or manager, on a Wakalah or Mudarabah basis, to operate the business. The operator and policy-taking participants contribute to the Takaful fund; claims are paid from this fund and the underwriting surplus or deficit is shared by the participants. In life (family) policies, part of the contribution is kept as an investment fund, with the operator using funds in business on a Wakalah or Mudarabah basis; the underwriting surplus/deficit belongs to the policyholders/partners, while investment-profit distribution depends on whether the Wakalah or Mudarabah basis applies. The modus operandi divides into family Takaful (life policies) and general Takaful. For family Takaful, the life policyholder's contribution splits into a 'protection part' (funding the Takaful fund/claims) and a savings/investment part if the company works as Mudarib; if the company works on a Wakalah basis, contributions split three ways - a management fee part, a protection part, and an investment part. The protection part operates on the donation (Tabarru') principle, with individual rights given up in favour of the Waqf; in the investment/savings part, individual rights remain intact under the Mudarabah principle, with contributions plus profit (net of expenses) paid to policyholders at the end of the policy term or earlier if required. For general Takaful, the whole contribution is treated as a protection donation, participants relinquish ownership rights in favour of the Takaful fund, and the UWS/UWL belongs to the participants; the company provides Qard al Hasan to the fund if claims exceed available funds/reserves at any point. Takaful companies can arrange re-Takaful on the same bases (Tabarru', Waqf, Mudarabah), paying an agreed contribution from the Takaful fund to a re-Takaful operator, who assists in case of losses. On the treatment of Tabarru': not every donation is equivalent to Sadaqah (charity, which can neither be taken back nor benefited from once given) - operators working on the Mudarabah model view the Takaful fund as a separate legal entity, with the protection-part contribution conditional on being used to pay claims, and any surplus potentially returned to participants (proportionate ownership of the contribution remains with participants to the extent funds are unused for claims). Where the amount is treated as Tabarru' from the outset (the Waqf model), it is used for the Waqf's beneficiaries; like Sadaqah, the donor relinquishes individual ownership, but unlike Sadaqah, the donor can still benefit as one of the beneficiaries - this is why the Waqf-based model, introduced more recently, is considered preferable. The whole contribution (or a part of it) becomes a donation to the Waqf fund; policyholders have no claim on the donation part used for claims payment, while the operator invests the funds and shares profits with the Waqf fund, and policyholders receive any profit share as fund beneficiaries. Historically, early Takaful operators made no distinction between underwriting surplus and 'investment profit', with surplus/profit sharing only after fulfilling the obligation of assisting fellow participants; ongoing scholarly research has settled that the whole UWS/UWL should belong to participants/policyholders, with the Takaful operator earning only a Takaful fee and/or a share of the 'investment profit'.",
