      id: "ch16-t5",
      sectionNumber: "16.4.1",
      sectionTitle: "Models of Takaful",
      title: "Models of Takaful: Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf",
      overview: "Scholars have proposed several Takaful operating models, with the Wakalah-Waqf combination - per Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani's research - identified as the best basis for a practical, Shari'ah-compliant Takaful system.",
      simpleExplanation: "Different regions run Takaful differently - the Middle East mostly uses a pure Wakalah (agency-fee) model, Asia-Pacific mostly uses a pure Mudarabah (profit-sharing) model, and there are combined Mudarabah-Wakalah models too - but after extensive research, over forty scholars concluded that a Waqf-based model, or a combination of Wakalah and Waqf, works best because it avoids most of the fairness problems the other models have.",
      academicExplanation: "Per the latest research by over forty Shari'ah scholars under the guidance of Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani (a renowned contemporary jurist, member of the Shari'ah councils of the OIC/IDB and AAOIFI), a Waqf model or a combination of Wakalah and Waqf is the best basis for a practical Shari'ah-compliant Takaful system; some jurists had earlier advocated a Waqf mechanism even before this research. In a pure Wakalah model (generally practised in the Middle East), the operator acts as Wakil for participants and earns a fee as an agreed percentage (e.g. 30%) of participants' donations, with the whole UWS/UWL and investment profit/loss belonging to the policyholders/participants; the Wakalah fee covers all management expenses and is fixed annually in advance with the company's Shari'ah committee; a part of UWS may be given to the operator as a performance incentive, though UWL is borne only by participants (the operator simply provides Qard al Hasan) - a feature Shari'ah scholars have found inequitable. In a pure Mudarabah model (mainly practised in Asia-Pacific), participants and the operator enter a Mudarabah contract for cooperative loss-sharing and profit-sharing; profit (investment returns plus any underwriting surplus) is distributed per a mutually agreed ratio (e.g. 50:50, 60:40) approved annually by the Shari'ah committee, with most expenses charged to shareholders. This model has an issue: Tabarru'-donated amounts cannot simultaneously serve as Mudarabah capital, and the operator gets UWS but does not bear UWL, prompting serious Shari'ah objections. A combined Mudarabah-Wakalah model shares both UWS and investment profit between participants and operators as a contractual entitlement, but has a structural issue: under Mudarabah, the operator (as Mudarib) cannot charge management expenses separately from its Mudarib share, whereas under Wakalah the operator (as agent) can take management fees from the fund per pre-agreed terms - and the operator still does not bear UWL, so this model also faces Shari'ah concerns. In the Waqf model, shareholders create a Waqf fund (Takaful fund) via an initial donation; more than one fund can exist for different service classes. Participant contributions (risk-appropriate) split into a donation to the Takaful fund and an investment portion on a Mudarabah basis; the donation part always remains with the Waqf, and operational costs (re-Takaful, claims) are met from the fund. The underwriting surplus/loss belongs to the fund, which may be distributed to Waqf beneficiaries, kept as reserve, or reinvested - there is no obligation to distribute the surplus. Management fees, profit distribution rules, reserve creation, and compensation extent/limits are all decided beforehand; shareholders provide Qard al Hasan to the fund if needed. A Mudarabah contract between the Takaful fund and the company (as Mudarib) governs investment, with the investment part redeemed to the policyholder on a NAV basis at policy maturity, and investment profit shared between company and fund. Box 16.1 details the Wakalah-with-Waqf model flowchart: (1) shareholders create a Waqf fund via initial donation; policyholders/participants then donate to the fund; (2) the company invests 70-75% of the Waqf fund on a Wakalah basis, with 25-30% retained as its management fee to cover all management expenses; (3) claims/operational costs of Takaful and re-Takaful are charged to the fund, with UWS/UWL belonging to participants as a group; (4) a Mudarabah contract for investment purposes operates between the fund and company, with an example profit-sharing ratio of 60% (fund) to 40% (company), though this can be any agreed ratio. Besides technical reserves, the Waqf fund may create a contingency reserve fund from contributions and investment profit, itself Waqf property; the company's income sources are the Waqf management fee, a share of investment profit as Mudarib or a service charge as investment agent, and profit from shareholders' own money.",
